WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
Module 18 is the shim seam — the architectural decision that let the browser build in E54 exist without forking a single UI file.

2
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.000
Today we open the source and see exactly how.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.783
Remember from E54: the desktop app never talks to Rust directly.

4
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:15.609
Every touch point goes through five imports from @tauri-apps — core, plugin-fs, plugin-http, api/event, plugin-opener, plugin-updater.

5
00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:19.000
Everything the app does to the outside world funnels through those five names.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.615
web/shims holds a browser file for each one, with the same exported function names and signatures.

7
00:00:20.615 --> 00:00:23.284
The Vite config for the web build aliases the real package name to the shim path — so every import in the app source is completely unaware which one it's getting.

8
00:00:23.284 --> 00:00:23.366
core.

9
00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:24.157
ts is the dispatcher standing in for invoke; fs.

10
00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:25.294
ts is the Origin Private File System standing in for plugin-fs; http.

11
00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:26.859
ts is just native fetch, because a Rust client isn't bound by CORS but a browser tab is; event.

12
00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.260
ts is a local, same-window bus since nothing in a tab emits from a Rust side; opener.

13
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.150
ts opens a real tab instead of asking the OS; updater.

14
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.484
ts just reports no update, because a page reload is the update mechanism; secret.

15
00:00:30.484 --> 00:00:32.000
ts trades the OS keychain for a namespaced slice of localStorage, plainly labeled as weaker.

16
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:32.170
core.

17
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.029
ts is the interesting one, because it isn't a one-to-one swap — it's a small router.

18
00:00:35.029 --> 00:00:36.798
A handlers object maps a command name to a function.

19
00:00:36.798 --> 00:00:45.000
invoke looks the name up: found, it runs it; not found, it throws a clear, specific reason instead of a generic failure, so a caller with a fallback — like detectOs falling back to the user agent — can degrade gracefully instead of crashing.

20
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.659
And that map is built up, not hardcoded — registerCommand adds an entry to it.

21
00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:52.091
Every other shim file that needs to answer a Tauri command, not just replace an import, calls registerCommand once at module load.

22
00:00:52.091 --> 00:00:52.330
secret.

23
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:57.000
ts is the clean example: it registers secret_set, secret_get and secret_delete, each just reading or writing localStorage under a prefix.

24
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.958
That's the whole trick, stated plainly: a new capability for the web build is a new file in web/shims that imports registerCommand and calls it a few times.

25
00:01:02.958 --> 00:01:08.000
Nothing in src/lib or src/components changes, because they were already calling invoke with that command name for the desktop build.

26
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.477
Gotcha: not every command needs a shim.

27
00:01:09.477 --> 00:01:09.667
core.

28
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:14.780
ts also keeps an UNAVAILABLE map of commands that are honestly impossible in a tab — start_server, mcp_connect, mesh_start, inapp_open.

29
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.000
Those fail with a specific, useful reason on purpose, rather than pretending to work.

30
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.880
Time to see this pattern used for real, on the biggest thing added behind the seam so far: an actual Linux kernel, booted inside the browser tab.

31
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.000
Next episode walks the source and the commit history behind it.

